  ],
  "reply": "No, not at the current pace. I took the checkout epic to be SYN-1 Checkout v2: phone wallets and saved cards. This thread's workstream is empty, so I found the epic with a search outside it. I can only read it from here, not change it.\n\nData is current to 29 October, which leaves two days in the month. The epic has 14 items. 5 are done, 4 are in progress and 5 haven't started, so 9 are left. Since early September the team has finished a bit under one item a week. At that rate, Oriel's estimate is that the rest will be done around **mid-January**, and most likely between late December and mid-March. That assumes no new work gets added. The chart below has the full figures.\n\n**What's slowing it down:**\n- **Some to-do items haven't moved in about eight weeks.** SYN-88 Refund to the original payment method when a sailing is cancelled, SYN-98 Apply promo codes at checkout, SYN-99 Save a card for next time, SYN-57 Checkout analytics: drop-off by step and SYN-10 Remove the legacy web-view checkout haven't been touched since early September.\n- **One in-progress item has been stuck for three weeks.** SYN-21 Update the payment declined error message has been in progress for 22 days.\n- **One person has a lot on.** Amara Okafor has two items in progress (SYN-92 Show the fare breakdown before payment and SYN-23 Pay with a phone wallet on the website) and another waiting to start.\n- **Some sizes are missing.** SYN-23 Pay with a phone wallet on the website, SYN-99 Save a card for next time and SYN-104 Handle 3-D Secure challenges in the native checkout have no estimate, so the remaining work could be bigger than it looks.\n\nIf the epic has to land soon, the only real options are cutting scope or adding people. I can put the epic and its items into this workstream so you can follow them here."
}

Claims:
1. The reply gives no finish date, rate or forecast of its own: any it gives is the one Oriel's block caption states, or it says Oriel had too little history to forecast. A yes or no to the question that follows from Oriel's forecast is not one of its own.
